notes               = "Untitled in source. One of a matched Isfahan pair with oldest-bridge (page 97) — the moon, the one old tree, the Zayandeh-rud — printed out of date order: this page is dated 11/22 and the next 11/21. The book's ordering, not a transcription slip. Line 13 is genuinely ambiguous: تو با ماهی is either 'you are with the moon' (ماه + copula) or 'you are with the fish' (ماهی); rendered as moon to match the poem's own lantern-of-the-moon image, with the river making fish readable too. برخیزم, 'let me rise', opens this poem and closes not-having (108–109). ۱۳۶۸/۱۱/۲۲ is 22 Bahman = 11 February 1990. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

برخیزم
فانوسِ ماه را
بر تک درختِ پیر بیاویزم
و آبِ چشمه را
در قوری قدیمی
بر آتش بگذارم...
مرغی در ساحل می‌خواند
و... دستِ ماه
بر سینهٔ زاینده‌رود می‌لرزد...
من چون آتش گرم...
و... چون رود لرزانم
و با یادت آواز می‌خوانم...
تو با ماهی...
تو از طلوعِ آتش
در راهی...
اصفهان ۱۳۶۸/۱۱/۲۲

===machine===

Let me rise
and hang the lantern of the moon
on the one old tree
and set the spring water
in an old teapot
on the fire...
A bird is singing on the shore
and... the hand of the moon
trembles on the breast of the Zayandeh-rud...
I am warm as fire...
and... trembling as the river
and with the memory of you I sing...
You are with the moon...
you are on your way
out of the rising of the fire...
Isfahan 1368/11/22

===lantern===

Let me rise
and hang the lantern of the moon
on the one old tree,
and set the spring water
in an old teapot
on the fire...
A bird is singing on the shore
and... the hand of the moon
trembles on the breast of the Zayandeh-rud...
I am warm as the fire,
and... trembling as the river,
and I sing with the memory of you...
You are with the moon...
you are on the road
out of the fire's rising...

Isfahan 1368/11/22

===translation===

===footnotes===

The Zayandeh-rud is Isfahan's river, and this poem and the next are a pair made
out of the same four things: the moon, the river, one old tree, and a bridge. The moon is hung on
the tree like a lantern here and called the lantern of lovers on the facing page.

تو با ماهی can be read two ways and the Persian does not choose: *you are with the moon* (ماه plus
the copula) or *you are with the fish*. Moon is taken here because the poem has just hung one in a
tree — but a poem sitting on the bank of the Zayandeh-rud can mean fish, and the ambiguity may be
the point.

